1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,362
Williams Obstetrics.

2
00:00:02,312 --> 00:00:06,000
Chapter 63: Neurological Disorders.

3
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:24,362
Central Nervous System Imaging Headache Seizure Disorders Cerebrovascular Diseases Demyelinating Or Degenerative Diseases Neuropathies Spinal Cord Injury Idiopathic Intracranial Hypertension Maternal Ventricular Shunts Maternal Brain Death References Many neurological disorders precede pregnancy.

4
00:00:24,362 --> 00:00:31,600
Most affected women will have successful perinatal outcomes, although some disorders carry specific risks.

5
00:00:31,600 --> 00:00:39,825
Other women will have new-onset neurological symptoms during pregnancy, and these must be distinguished from pregnancy complications.

6
00:00:39,825 --> 00:00:51,387
Neurovascular disorders are an important cause of maternal mortality and accounted for more than 7 percent of maternal deaths in the United States from 2007 through 2016 .

7
00:00:51,387 --> 00:01:00,825
Psychiatric disorders can manifest with cognitive and neuromuscular abnormalities and should be considered in the evaluation of neurological symptoms.

8
00:01:00,825 --> 00:01:03,487
Central Nervous System Imaging.

9
00:01:03,487 --> 00:01:14,525
Computed tomography (CT) and magnetic resonance (MR) imaging assist in the diagnosis, classification, and management of many neurological disorders.

10
00:01:14,525 --> 00:01:25,175
As discussed in Chapter 49, (p. 873), these can be used safely during pregnancy (Chansakul, 2017; Lum, 2020).

11
00:01:25,175 --> 00:01:32,525
CT scanning is often used when rapid diagnosis is necessary and is excellent for detecting recent hemorrhage.

12
00:01:32,525 --> 00:01:49,712
That said, MR imaging is often preferred and is particularly helpful to diagnose demyelinating diseases, arteriovenous malformations (AVMs), congenital and developmental nervous system abnormalities, posterior fossa lesions, and spinal cord diseases .

13
00:01:49,712 --> 00:02:02,400
Whenever either modality is employed, a woman with advanced pregnancy should be positioned in a lateral tilt with a wedge under one hip to prevent hypotension and to diminish aortic pulsations, which may degrade the image.

14
00:02:02,400 --> 00:02:13,500
Cerebral angiography with contrast injection, usually via the femoral artery, is a valuable adjunct to the diagnosis and treatment of some cerebrovascular diseases.

15
00:02:13,500 --> 00:02:18,950
Fluoroscopy delivers more radiation but can be performed with abdominal shielding.

16
00:02:18,950 --> 00:02:30,700
Positron emission tomography (PET) and functional MR imaging (fMRI) have not been evaluated in detail for use in gravidas (van den Heuvel, 2016).

17
00:02:30,700 --> 00:02:32,250
Headache.

18
00:02:32,250 --> 00:02:44,075
In one national survey in the United States in 2012, 17 percent of persons aged 18 to 44 years reported a severe headache or migraine within the past 3 months .

19
00:02:44,075 --> 00:02:52,437
Burch and coworkers (2015) reported that 24 percent of nonpregnant women in this age group were similarly affected.

20
00:02:52,437 --> 00:03:00,275
Of pregnant women presenting with headache who received a neurological consultation, two thirds were due to primary disorders.

21
00:03:00,275 --> 00:03:04,075
The diagnosis was migraine in more than 90 percent.

22
00:03:04,075 --> 00:03:10,400
Of the other third due to secondary conditions, more than half stemmed from hypertensive disorders .

23
00:03:10,400 --> 00:03:21,387
In one recent observational study at University of Texas Southwestern’s Clements University Hospital, 20 percent of emergency department postpartum visits were for headache .

24
00:03:21,387 --> 00:03:29,250
The classification by the International Headache Society (2018) is shown in Table 63-1.

25
00:03:29,250 --> 00:03:36,412
As discussed, in pregnant women, primary headaches are more common than those derived from secondary causes .

26
00:03:36,412 --> 00:03:42,212
Migraine headaches are those most likely to be affected by the hormonal changes of pregnancy .

27
00:03:42,212 --> 00:03:49,275
The incidences of different etiologies of severe headaches in pregnancy are shown in Figure 63-1 .

28
00:03:49,275 --> 00:03:51,862
Table 63-1.

29
00:03:51,862 --> 00:03:55,650
Headache Classification Figure 63-1.

30
00:03:55,650 --> 00:04:03,837
Incidences of headache causes in 140 consecutive pregnant women for whom inhospital neurology consultation was requested.

31
00:04:03,837 --> 00:04:06,325
# Tension Headache.

32
00:04:06,325 --> 00:04:09,662
These are the most frequent cause of all headaches.

33
00:04:09,662 --> 00:04:12,512
They may be episodic or chronic.

34
00:04:12,512 --> 00:04:19,525
Their characteristic muscle tightness and mild to moderate pain in the back of the neck and head can persist for hours.

35
00:04:19,525 --> 00:04:26,900
Importantly, neurological disturbances or nausea are typically absent, which distinguishes them from migraines.

36
00:04:26,900 --> 00:04:36,962
The pain usually responds to rest, massage, local heat or ice, nonsteroidal antiinflammatory drugs (NSAIDs), or mild tranquilizers.

37
00:04:36,962 --> 00:04:40,100
Hospital admission is seldom necessary.

38
00:04:40,100 --> 00:04:44,112
For chronic headaches, amitriptyline can be effective .

39
00:04:44,112 --> 00:04:46,612
# Migraine Headache.

40
00:04:46,612 --> 00:04:48,300
This is a

41
00:04:48,460 --> 00:05:01,560
periodic, sometimes incapacitating neurological disorder with episodic attacks of severe headache and autonomic nervous system dysfunction (Ashina, 2020; Charles, 2017).

42
00:05:01,510 --> 00:05:11,272
The International Headache Society (2018) classifies three migraine types based on chronicity and on the presence or absence of an aura.

43
00:05:11,272 --> 00:05:21,247
1. Migraine without aura-formerly termed common migraine-is characterized by a unilateral throbbing headache, nausea and vomiting, or photophobia.

44
00:05:21,247 --> 00:05:32,097
2. Migraine with aura-formerly termed classic migraine-has similar symptoms preceded by premonitory neurological phenomena such as visual scotoma or hallucinations.

45
00:05:32,097 --> 00:05:39,522
A third of patients have this type, which sometimes can be averted if medication is taken at the first premonitory sign.

46
00:05:39,522 --> 00:05:47,147
3. Chronic migraine is defined by a migraine headache occurring at least 15 days each month for more than 3 months.

47
00:05:47,147 --> 00:05:56,185
Migraine headaches may begin in childhood, peak in adolescence, and tend to diminish in both frequency and severity with advancing years.

48
00:05:56,185 --> 00:06:03,785
According to Ashina (2020), their annual prevalence is 15 percent in women and 6 percent in men.

49
00:06:03,785 --> 00:06:10,097
Another 5 percent of women have probable migraine, that is, they have all criteria but one.

50
00:06:10,097 --> 00:06:19,010
These headaches are especially common in young women and have been linked to hormone levels (Burch, 2020; Charles, 2017).

51
00:06:19,010 --> 00:06:28,360
Sensory sensitivity with migraines is likely caused by monoaminergic sensory control systems in the brainstem, hypothalamus, and cortex .

52
00:06:28,360 --> 00:06:42,822
The exact pathophysiology is uncertain, but symptoms develop when neuronal dysfunction leads to decreased cortical blood flow, activation of vascular and meningeal nociceptors, and stimulation of trigeminal sensory neurons.

53
00:06:42,822 --> 00:06:47,535
Importantly, migraines are not primarily a vascular disorder .

54
00:06:47,535 --> 00:06:55,210
Migraine headaches—especially those with aura in young women—are associated with increased risk for ischemic strokes .

55
00:06:55,210 --> 00:07:01,635
Other associated conditions are asthma, anxiety and depression, and other pain disorders.

56
00:07:01,635 --> 00:07:07,035
Migraine in Pregnancy Migraine headaches are frequently encountered during pregnancy.

57
00:07:07,035 --> 00:07:10,735
Most migraineurs have improvement during pregnancy .

58
00:07:10,735 --> 00:07:17,447
Still, migraines—usually those with an aura—occasionally appear for the first time during pregnancy.

59
00:07:17,447 --> 00:07:28,047
Pregnant women with preexisting migraine symptoms may have other symptoms that suggest a more serious disorder, and new neurological symptoms should prompt a complete evaluation .

60
00:07:28,047 --> 00:07:44,772
Migraines during pregnancy are associated with increased rates of preeclampsia, gestational hypertension, preterm birth, and other cardiovascular morbidities that include ischemic stroke (Grossman, 2017; Wabnitz, 2015).

61
00:07:44,772 --> 00:07:54,772
Bushnell and associates (2009) identified a migraine incidence during pregnancy of 185 cases per 100,000 deliveries.

62
00:07:54,772 --> 00:08:12,422
Of diagnoses associated in these gravid migraineurs, risks were significantly higher for stroke, 16-fo1d; myocardial infarction, fivefold; and heart disease, venous thromboembolism, and preeclampsia/gestational hypertension, each twofold.

63
00:08:12,422 --> 00:08:20,335
In a systematic review of 14 studies, risks for preeclampsia and low-birthweight neonates were significantly increased .

64
00:08:20,335 --> 00:08:25,747
Management Several effective medications are shown in Table 63-2.

65
00:08:25,747 --> 00:08:31,097
Mild headaches respond to oral therapy in 50 to 70 percent of cases.

66
00:08:31,097 --> 00:08:39,135
Initial therapy with acetaminophen or an NSAID, along with an antiemetic, usually suffices for many early headaches.

67
00:08:39,135 --> 00:08:43,335
For severe headaches, parenteral therapy is indicated .

68
00:08:43,335 --> 00:08:48,785
Multitargeted drug therapy is necessary in most cases for migraine relief .

69
00:08:48,785 --> 00:08:59,022
Headaches are treated aggressively with intravenous hydration, parenteral antiemetics, and triptans, and opioids are reserved for the most recalcitrant headaches.

70
00:08:59,022 --> 00:09:07,535
Although a 2-g infusion of magnesium sulfate has gained favor in the past few years, a metaanalysis reported no benefits .

71
00:09:07,535 --> 00:09:14,797
Ergotamine derivatives are potent vasoconstrictors and are avoided in pregnancy because of their uterotonic effects .

72
00:09:14,797 --> 00:09:17,360
Table 63-2.

73
00:09:17,360 --> 00:09:31,685
Medications for Treatment of Acute Migraine in Pregnancy Triptans are serotonin 5-HT1B/2D-receptor agonists that effectively relieve headaches, nausea, and vomiting and greatly reduce analgesic needs.

74
00:09:31,685 --> 00:09:34,272
Triptans can be given as an

75
00:09:34,444 --> 00:09:39,731
oral tablet, injection, rectal suppository, or intranasal spray.

76
00:09:39,681 --> 00:09:43,206
They are best used in combination with NSAIDs .

77
00:09:43,206 --> 00:09:54,006
However, chronic NSAID use is avoided in pregnancy, especially later pregnancy because of fetal ductus arteriosus constriction and fetal urine production decline .

78
00:09:54,006 --> 00:10:07,131
The greatest triptan experience is with sumatriptan (Imitrex), and although not studied extensively in pregnancy, it is not a major teratogen (Saldanha, 2021; Spielmann, 2018).

79
00:10:07,131 --> 00:10:20,869
However, in one study at 36 months of children exposed to triptans in pregnancy, Wood and colleagues (2016) found neurodevelopment differences, including emotionality and activity problems.

80
00:10:20,869 --> 00:10:31,444
Some women will benefit from peripheral nerve blocks, and Govindappagari and coworkers (2014) described their experiences with 13 pregnant women.

81
00:10:31,444 --> 00:10:37,044
For women with frequent migraine headaches, oral prophylactic therapy is warranted.

82
00:10:37,044 --> 00:10:57,356
Amitriptyline (Elavil), 10 to 175 mg daily; propranolol (Inderal), 40 to 120 mg daily; or metoprolol (Lopressor, Toprol), 25 to 100 mg daily, are successful options (Contag, 2010; Lucas, 2009).

83
00:10:57,356 --> 00:11:05,431
Calcitonin gene-related peptide (CGRP) receptor antagonists to prevent migraines have garnered enthusiasm.

84
00:11:05,431 --> 00:11:16,969
Several of these monoclonal antibodies have been approved since 2018 for clinical use but not evaluated in pregnancy (Ashina, 2020; Tepper, 2018).

85
00:11:16,969 --> 00:11:26,569
Similarly, in a Cochrane review, botulinum toxin type A for chronic migraine may reduce migraine days per month by 2 days compared with placebo .

86
00:11:26,569 --> 00:11:29,031
# Cluster Headache.

87
00:11:29,031 --> 00:11:42,794
This rare primary headache disorder is characterized by severe unilateral lancinating pain radiating to the face and orbit, lasting 15 to 180 minutes, and occurring with autonomic symptoms and agitation.

88
00:11:42,794 --> 00:11:46,081
Pregnancy does not affect symptom severity.

89
00:11:46,081 --> 00:11:49,656
Affected women should avoid tobacco and alcohol.

90
00:11:49,656 --> 00:11:57,956
Acute management includes 100-percent oxygen therapy and sumatriptan given as a 6-mg dose subcutaneously .

91
00:11:57,956 --> 00:12:01,794
If recurrent, prophylaxis employs verapamil.

92
00:12:01,794 --> 00:12:03,856
Seizure Disorders.

93
00:12:03,856 --> 00:12:10,269
After headaches, seizures are the next most prevalent neurological condition encountered in pregnancy.

94
00:12:10,269 --> 00:12:17,756
The Centers for Disease Control and Prevention reported that 3 million adults in 2015 had active epilepsy .

95
00:12:17,756 --> 00:12:21,106
Less than half were seizure free in the past year.

96
00:12:21,106 --> 00:12:29,569
Importantly, epilepsy accounted for 5 percent of maternal deaths in the United Kingdom from 2011 to 2013 .

97
00:12:29,569 --> 00:12:32,369
# Pathophysiology.

98
00:12:32,369 --> 00:12:40,494
A seizure is a transient combination of signs and symptoms stemming from abnormal excessive or synchronous neuronal activity in the brain .

99
00:12:40,494 --> 00:12:55,831
Some identifiable causes of convulsive disorders in young adults include head trauma, alcohol- and other drug-induced withdrawals, cerebral infections, brain tumors, biochemical abnormalities, and arteriovenous malformations.

100
00:12:55,831 --> 00:13:00,906
A search for these is prudent with a newonset seizure disorder in a pregnant woman.

101
00:13:00,906 --> 00:13:05,381
The diagnosis of idiopathic epilepsy is one of exclusion.

102
00:13:05,381 --> 00:13:07,831
# Classification.

103
00:13:07,831 --> 00:13:15,106
Epilepsy encompasses different syndromes whose cardinal feature is a predisposition to recurrent unprovoked seizures.

104
00:13:15,106 --> 00:13:20,656
The International League Against Epilepsy recently updated the following definitions .

105
00:13:20,656 --> 00:13:27,244
Main categories describe seizure onset and include focal, generalized, and unknown groups.

106
00:13:27,244 --> 00:13:36,369
Focal seizure onset describes those that originate in one localized brain area and affect correspondingly localized neurological function.

107
00:13:36,369 --> 00:13:44,969
They are believed to result from trauma, abscess, tumor, or perinatal factors, although a specific lesion is rarely demonstrated.

108
00:13:44,969 --> 00:13:49,131
Seizures may occur with either intact or impaired awareness.

109
00:13:49,131 --> 00:13:59,344
Focal seizures without dyscognitive features start in one region of the body and progress toward other ipsilateral areas of the body and produce tonic and then clonic movements.

110
00:13:59,344 --> 00:14:05,769
Seizures can affect sensory function or produce autonomic dysfunction or psychological changes.

111
00:14:05,769 --> 00:14:10,081
Cognitive function is not impaired, and recovery is rapid.

112
00:14:10,081 --> 00:14:20,194
Focal seizures with dyscognitive features are often preceded by an aura and followed by impaired awareness manifested by sudden behavioral arrest or motionless stare.

113
00:14:20,194 --> 00:14:25,131
Involuntary movements such as picking motions or lip smacking are common.

114
00:14:25,131 --> 00:14:27,256
Generalized seizure

115
00:14:27,412 --> 00:14:35,274
onset describes those that involve both brain hemispheres and may be preceded by an aura before an abrupt loss of consciousness.

116
00:14:35,224 --> 00:14:37,762
They may be motor or nonmotor.

117
00:14:37,762 --> 00:14:40,799
There is a strong hereditary component.

118
00:14:40,799 --> 00:14:53,787
In generalized tonic-clonic seizures, loss of consciousness is followed by tonic contraction of the muscles and rigid posturing, and then by clonic contractions of all extremities while the muscles gradually relax.

119
00:14:53,787 --> 00:15:00,762
Return to consciousness is gradual, and the patient may remain confused and disoriented for several hours.

120
00:15:00,762 --> 00:15:14,574
Absence seizures—also called petit mal seizures—are a form of generalized epilepsy that involve a brief loss of consciousness without muscle activity and are characterized by immediate recovery of consciousness and orientation.

121
00:15:14,574 --> 00:15:19,324
Unknown seizure onset describes seizures with an unwitnessed onset.

122
00:15:19,324 --> 00:15:27,262
Thus, they remain unclassified as to focal or generalized onset until further information allows categorization.

123
00:15:27,262 --> 00:15:32,974
However, these can be further subgrouped as motor or nonmotor, or unclassified.

124
00:15:32,974 --> 00:15:35,987
# Preconceptional Counseling.

125
00:15:35,987 --> 00:15:45,337
Women with epilepsy ideally are counseled before pregnancy, and relevant points are presented also in Chapter 9 (p. 166).

126
00:15:45,337 --> 00:15:52,974
Oral folic acid supplementation with 0.4 mg per day is begun at least 1 month before conception.

127
00:15:52,974 --> 00:15:59,537
The dose is increased to 4 mg when the woman taking antiepileptic medication becomes pregnant.

128
00:15:59,537 --> 00:16:06,462
These medications are assessed and adjusted with a goal of monotherapy using the least teratogenic medication.

129
00:16:06,462 --> 00:16:17,587
If this is not feasible, attempts are made to reduce the number of medications and to use the lowest effective dose (Stephen, 2019; Tomson, 2019b).

130
00:16:17,587 --> 00:16:23,162
Medication withdrawal should be considered if a woman is seizure free for 2 years or more.

131
00:16:23,162 --> 00:16:26,174
# Pregnancy Complications.

132
00:16:26,174 --> 00:16:32,924
The major pregnancy-related risks to women with epilepsy are fetal malformations and convulsions.

133
00:16:32,924 --> 00:16:38,112
Seizure control is a priority to avoid its attendant morbidity and mortality.

134
00:16:38,112 --> 00:16:46,649
Early studies described worsening seizure activity during pregnancy, however, this is less common now because of more effective drugs .

135
00:16:46,649 --> 00:16:53,449
Contemporary studies cite higher rates of seizure activity in only 20 to 30 percent of gravidas .

136
00:16:53,449 --> 00:17:00,049
Women who are seizure free for at least 9 months before conception will likely remain so during pregnancy .

137
00:17:00,049 --> 00:17:08,649
Greater seizure frequency is often associated with subtherapeutic anticonvulsant serum levels, a lower seizure threshold, or both.

138
00:17:08,649 --> 00:17:14,649
Numerous pregnancy-associated alterations can result in subtherapeutic serum levels.

139
00:17:14,649 --> 00:17:36,362
These include nausea and vomiting, slower gastrointestinal motility, antacid use that diminishes drug absorption, pregnancy hypervolemia offset by protein binding, induction of hepatic enzymes such as cytochrome oxidases, placental enzymes that metabolize drugs, and increased glomerular filtration that hastens drug clearance.

140
00:17:36,362 --> 00:17:42,274
Importantly, some women discontinue medication because of teratogenicity concerns.

141
00:17:42,274 --> 00:17:50,524
Last, the seizure threshold can be affected by pregnancy-related sleep deprivation and by hyperventilation and pain during labor .

142
00:17:50,524 --> 00:17:54,099
Status epilepticus is a medical emergency.

143
00:17:54,099 --> 00:17:58,374
It is more likely to occur in women with refractory epilepsy .

144
00:17:58,374 --> 00:18:10,849
In a randomized trial of 384 nonpregnant patients with benzodiazepine-refractory status, intravenous levetiracetam, fosphenytoin, or valproate yielded similar results .

145
00:18:10,849 --> 00:18:17,824
Only one half of patients in each cohort had cessation of seizures and improved alertness by 60 minutes.

146
00:18:17,824 --> 00:18:38,587
In addition to seizure-control challenges, women with epilepsy have a small increased risk of pregnancy complications that include miscarriage, hemorrhage, hypertensive disorders, placental abruption, preterm birth, fetalgrowth restriction, and cesarean delivery (Salman, 2018; Viale, 2015).

147
00:18:38,587 --> 00:18:51,374
Importantly, MacDonald (2015) also reports a tenfold higher maternal death rate, and, as mentioned earlier, epilepsy accounted for 5 percent of maternal deaths in the United Kingdom.

148
00:18:51,374 --> 00:18:55,924
Postpartum depression rates also are increased in epileptic women .

149
00:18:55,924 --> 00:19:01,962
Last, children of epileptic mothers have a 10-percent risk of developing a seizure disorder.

150
00:19:01,962 --> 00:19:06,462
With embryofetal malformations, it was difficult for years to

151
00:19:06,628 --> 00:19:11,978
separate the influence of epilepsy from that of its therapy as the primary cause.

152
00:19:11,928 --> 00:19:18,428
Now, untreated epilepsy is thought not to be associated with an elevated fetal malformation rate .

153
00:19:18,428 --> 00:19:28,065
That said, the fetus of an epileptic mother who takes certain anticonvulsant medications has an indisputably greater risk for congenital malformations.

154
00:19:28,065 --> 00:19:35,203
Moreover, monotherapy is associated with a lower rate of birth defects compared with multiagent therapy.

155
00:19:35,203 --> 00:19:41,640
Adherence to these principles has resulted in declining malformation rates from antiepileptic drugs .

156
00:19:41,640 --> 00:19:46,328
Specific drugs, when given alone, raise the malformation rate .

157
00:19:46,328 --> 00:19:49,815
Some are listed in Table 63-3.

158
00:19:49,815 --> 00:20:00,140
Phenytoin and phenobarbital increase the major malformation rate two- to threefold above baseline (Perucca, 2005; Thomas, 2008).

159
00:20:00,140 --> 00:20:12,628
Valproate is a particularly potent teratogen, which has a dose-dependent effect and raises the malformation risk four- to eightfold (Klein, 2014; Tomson, 2019b).

160
00:20:12,628 --> 00:20:17,178
Valproate is also associated with lower cognitive performance .

161
00:20:17,178 --> 00:20:22,065
In general, with polytherapy, the risk rises with each drug added.

162
00:20:22,065 --> 00:20:29,353
A metaanalysis of 31 studies found lamotrigine and levetiracetam to carry the lowest malformation risk .

163
00:20:29,353 --> 00:20:31,915
Table 63-3.

164
00:20:31,915 --> 00:20:38,215
Teratogenic Effects of Common Anticonvulsant Medications # Management in Pregnancy.

165
00:20:38,215 --> 00:20:48,653
The American Academy of Neurology and the American Epilepsy Society have guidelines regarding treatment in pregnancy (Harden, 2009a,b,c).

166
00:20:48,653 --> 00:20:51,540
The major goal is seizure prevention.

167
00:20:51,540 --> 00:21:01,128
To accomplish this, treatment for nausea and vomiting is provided, seizure-provoking stimuli are avoided, and medication compliance is emphasized.

168
00:21:01,128 --> 00:21:07,178
The fewest necessary anticonvulsants are given at the lowest dosage needed for seizure control.

169
00:21:07,178 --> 00:21:15,815
Although some providers routinely monitor serum drug levels, these concentrations may be unreliable because of altered protein binding.

170
00:21:15,815 --> 00:21:21,778
Free or unbound drug levels, although perhaps more accurate, are not widely available.

171
00:21:21,778 --> 00:21:27,428
Importantly, no evidence suggests that such monitoring improves seizure control .

172
00:21:27,428 --> 00:21:35,128
For these reasons, drug levels may be most informative if measured following seizures or if noncompliance is suspected.

173
00:21:35,128 --> 00:21:44,303
For women taking anticonvulsant drugs, a targeted sonographic examination in midpregnancy is recommended to evaluate for anomalies.

174
00:21:44,303 --> 00:21:51,065
Testing to assess fetal well-being is generally not indicated for women with uncomplicated epilepsy.

175
00:21:51,065 --> 00:21:58,428
For women desiring to breastfeed, data regarding the safety of the various anticonvulsant medications are limited.

176
00:21:58,428 --> 00:22:08,778
However, no obvious deleterious effects, such as long-term cognitive issues, have been reported (Birnbaum, 2020; Briggs, 2022).

177
00:22:08,778 --> 00:22:17,778
Of birth control methods, combination oral contraceptive (COC) pill failure rates are higher with some of the anticonvulsant agents.

178
00:22:17,778 --> 00:22:23,290
For example, lamotrigine efficacy is substantially lowered by COCs.

179
00:22:23,290 --> 00:22:27,215
Thus, other more reliable methods should be considered .

180
00:22:27,215 --> 00:22:29,953
Cerebrovascular Diseases.

181
00:22:29,953 --> 00:22:40,403
Abnormalities of the cerebrovascular circulation include both ischemic and hemorrhagic strokes and anatomical anomalies, such as AVMs and aneurysms.

182
00:22:40,403 --> 00:22:46,628
Cerebral ischemia is caused by reduction in blood flow that lasts longer than several seconds.

183
00:22:46,628 --> 00:22:50,253
Early, neurological symptoms may manifest.

184
00:22:50,253 --> 00:22:54,665
After a few minutes, however, infarction often follows.

185
00:22:54,665 --> 00:22:59,740
Hemorrhagic stroke is caused by bleeding directly into or around the brain.

186
00:22:59,740 --> 00:23:07,090
It produces symptoms by its mass effect, by blood’s toxic effects, or by elevated intracranial pressure.

187
00:23:07,090 --> 00:23:11,203
Strokes in nonpregnant women are predominately ischemic.

188
00:23:11,203 --> 00:23:16,465
In gravidas, roughly half are ischemic, and the other half are hemorrhagic .

189
00:23:16,465 --> 00:23:26,765
The obesity endemic in this country, along with a concomitant rise in rates of heart disease, hypertension, and diabetes, has increased the prevalence of strokes.

190
00:23:26,765 --> 00:23:37,240
Compared with men, women have a higher lifetime risk of stroke and associated mortality rate (Martinez-Sånchez, 2011; Virani, 2021).

191
00:23:37,240 --> 00:23:48,128
Moreover, pregnancy raises the immediate and lifetime risk of both ischemic stroke and hemorrhagic stroke (Jamieson, 2010; Jung, 2010).

192
00:23:48,128 --> 00:23:55,753
With an incidence of 10 to 40 cases per 100,000 births, stroke is relatively uncommon in gravidas.

193
00:23:55,753 --> 00:23:58,990
However, it contributes disparately to

194
00:23:59,140 --> 00:24:07,440
maternal mortality rates (Leffert, 2016; Pacheco, 2019; Elgendy, 2021).

195
00:24:07,390 --> 00:24:16,965
The incidence is rising as measured by pregnancy-related hospitalizations for stroke (Callaghan, 2008; Kuklina, 2011).

196
00:24:16,965 --> 00:24:21,990
Most events are associated with hypertensive disorders or heart disease.

197
00:24:21,990 --> 00:24:35,752
Of the pregnancy-related mortality rate in the United States from 2007 to 2016, 7.2 percent was due to cerebrovascular accidents, and 7.8 percent was associated with hypertension .

198
00:24:35,752 --> 00:24:42,502
Of maternal deaths after 42 days postpartum, 9.8 percent were attributable to stroke .

199
00:24:42,502 --> 00:24:44,890
# Risk Factors.

200
00:24:44,890 --> 00:24:50,815
Most strokes in pregnancy manifest either during labor and delivery or in the puerperium.

201
00:24:50,815 --> 00:25:02,090
In a study of 2850 pregnancy-related strokes, approximately 10 percent developed antepartum, 40 percent intrapartum, and 50 percent postpartum .

202
00:25:02,090 --> 00:25:14,065
Of 145 ischemic strokes, Leffert (2016) reported a timing of 45 percent antepartum, 3 percent intrapartum, and 53 percent postpartum.

203
00:25:14,065 --> 00:25:19,752
Several risk factors both unrelated and related to pregnancy have been recognized.

204
00:25:19,752 --> 00:25:32,927
Unrelated causes are age; migraines, hypertension, obesity, and diabetes; cardiac disorders such as endocarditis, valvular prostheses, and patent foramen ovale; and smoking.

205
00:25:32,927 --> 00:25:42,790
Stroke etiologies related to pregnancy include hypertensive disorders, gestational diabetes, obstetrical hemorrhage, and cesarean delivery.

206
00:25:42,790 --> 00:25:48,140
By far, the most common is pregnancy-associated hypertensive disorders.

207
00:25:48,140 --> 00:26:02,352
A third of strokes are associated with gestational hypertension, and hypertensive women compared with normotensive counterparts have a three- to eightfold greater risk of stroke (Scott, 2012; Wang, 2011).

208
00:26:02,352 --> 00:26:07,715
Stroke is the major cause of maternal mortality associated with preeclampsia .

209
00:26:07,715 --> 00:26:16,852
Moreover, women with preeclampsia undergoing general anesthesia may be at higher risk of stroke compared with those given neuraxial anesthesia .

210
00:26:16,852 --> 00:26:23,852
Cesarean delivery raises the peripartum stroke risk 1.5-fold compared with vaginal delivery .

211
00:26:23,852 --> 00:26:28,465
Sepsis from any source increases the risk for postpartum stroke .

212
00:26:28,465 --> 00:26:41,065
Pregnancy-induced effects on cerebrovascular hemodynamics include enhanced autoregulation that maintains blood flow despite changes in systemic blood pressure (van Teen, 2016).

213
00:26:41,065 --> 00:26:53,377
Although cerebral blood flow decreases by 20 percent from midpregnancy until term, it increases significantly with gestational hypertension (Zeeman, 2003, 2004b).

214
00:26:53,377 --> 00:26:59,690
Such hyperperfusion at least intuitively would be dangerous for women with certain vascular anomalies.

215
00:26:59,690 --> 00:27:02,202
# Ischemic Stroke.

216
00:27:02,202 --> 00:27:10,640
Acute occlusion or embolization of an intracranial blood vessel causes cerebral ischemia, which may result in death of brain tissue .

217
00:27:10,640 --> 00:27:17,340
The more commonly associated etiologies of ischemic stroke are shown in Table 63-4.

218
00:27:17,340 --> 00:27:26,315
A transient ischemic attack (TIA) is caused by reversible ischemia, and symptoms usually last less than 24 hours.

219
00:27:26,315 --> 00:27:30,902
Approximately 10 percent of these patients have a stroke by 1 year .

220
00:27:30,902 --> 00:27:33,477
Figure 63-2.

221
00:27:33,477 --> 00:27:54,640
Illustrations of a brain showing various types of strokes seen in pregnancy: (1) subcortical infarction (preeclampsia), (2) hypertensive hemorrhage, (3) aneurysm, (4) embolism or thrombosis in middle cerebral artery, (5) arteriovenous malformation, and (6) cortical vein thrombosis.

222
00:27:54,640 --> 00:27:57,302
Table 63-4.

223
00:27:57,302 --> 00:28:10,027
Some Associated Disorders or Causes of Ischemic and Hemorrhagic Strokes During Pregnancy or the Puerperium Stroke usually incites sudden severe headache, hemiplegia, or other neurological deficits.

224
00:28:10,027 --> 00:28:12,640
Seizures occasionally develop.

225
00:28:12,640 --> 00:28:16,527
Immediate assessment and CT scanning is indicated .

226
00:28:16,527 --> 00:28:24,940
Further evaluation includes echocardiography and additional cranial imaging with CT, MR imaging, or angiography.

227
00:28:24,940 --> 00:28:33,052
Serum lipids are measured with the caveat that their values are distorted by normal pregnancy (Appendix, p. 1231).

228
00:28:33,052 --> 00:28:43,402
Tests to detect antiphospholipid antibodies, lupus anticoagulant, other thrombophilias, and, if indicated, sickle-cell syndromes are performed .

229
00:28:43,402 --> 00:28:48,727
These underlie up to a third of ischemic strokes in otherwise healthy young women.

230
00:28:48,727 --> 00:28:53,977
With a thorough evaluation, most causes of embolism can be identified.

231
00:28:53,977 --> 00:29:06,177
Some of these include cardiac-associated embolism, vasculitis, or vasculopathy such as Moyamoya disease (Miyakoshi, 2009; Pacheco, 2019).

232
00:29:06,340 --> 00:29:12,627
Outcomes of embolic strokes are reported to be favorable and similar to those of nonpregnant women .

233
00:29:12,577 --> 00:29:18,002
For some, thrombolysis for ischemic stroke during pregnancy should be considered .

234
00:29:18,002 --> 00:29:21,590
However, published experiences are sparse .

235
00:29:21,590 --> 00:29:25,365
For some, mechanical thrombectomy can be used.

236
00:29:25,365 --> 00:29:39,677
Preeclampsia Syndrome In reproductive-age women, a significant proportion of pregnancy-related ischemic strokes are caused by gestational hypertension and preeclampsia (Miller, 2020; Zofkie, 2018).

237
00:29:39,677 --> 00:29:53,215
As shown in Figure 63-2, areas of subcortical edema and petechial hemorrhage may progress to cerebral infarction (Aukes, 2007, 2009; Zeeman, 2004a).

238
00:29:53,215 --> 00:30:02,265
Although these usually clinically manifest by an eclamptic convulsion, a few women will suffer a symptomatic stroke from a larger cortical infarction .

239
00:30:02,265 --> 00:30:11,215
Other conditions with findings similar to preeclampsia are thrombotic microangiopathies and the reversible cerebral vasoconstriction syndrome .

240
00:30:11,215 --> 00:30:25,165
The latter, also termed postpartum angiopathy, can cause extensive cerebral edema, necrosis and widespread infarction, and areas of hemorrhage (Edlow, 2013; Miller, 2016).

241
00:30:25,165 --> 00:30:32,327
Cerebral Embolism These strokes usually involve the middle cerebral artery (see Fig. 63-2).

242
00:30:32,327 --> 00:30:41,127
The diagnosis can be made with confidence only after thrombosis and hemorrhage are excluded and is more certain if an embolic source is identified.

243
00:30:41,127 --> 00:30:48,765
Hemorrhage may be more difficult to exclude because both embolization and thrombosis can be followed by hemorrhagic infarction.

244
00:30:48,765 --> 00:30:56,702
Paradoxical embolism is an uncommon cause, even considering that more than a fourth of adults have a patent foramen ovale.

245
00:30:56,702 --> 00:31:01,365
Through the defect, rightsided venous thromboemboli are deported .

246
00:31:01,365 --> 00:31:09,102
Foraminal closure may not improve outcomes in these patients, however, this procedure has been performed during pregnancy .

247
00:31:09,102 --> 00:31:23,540
Assorted cardioembolic causes of stroke include arrhythmias—especially atrial fibrillation, valvular lesions, mitral valve prolapse, mural thrombus, infective endocarditis, and peripartum cardiomyopathy.

248
00:31:23,540 --> 00:31:29,977
Management of embolic stroke in pregnancy consists of supportive measures and antiplatelet therapy.

249
00:31:29,977 --> 00:31:35,202
Thrombolytic therapy and anticoagulation in pregnancy are controversial .

250
00:31:35,202 --> 00:31:45,452
Cerebral Artery Thrombosis Most thrombotic strokes affect older individuals and are caused by atherosclerosis, especially of the internal carotid artery.

251
00:31:45,452 --> 00:31:49,102
Many are preceded by one or more TIAs.

252
00:31:49,102 --> 00:31:55,915
Thrombolytic therapy with a recombinant tissue plasminogen activator (rtPA) is recommended.

253
00:31:55,915 --> 00:31:58,265
Alteplase is one of these.

254
00:31:58,265 --> 00:32:06,777
It is given intravenously within the first 3-hour window if neurological deficits are noted and if neuroimaging has excluded hemorrhage .

255
00:32:06,777 --> 00:32:11,565
A principal risk is hemorrhagic transformation of an ischemic stroke.

256
00:32:11,565 --> 00:32:18,115
This agent can be used in pregnancy, but published experiences of rtPA in this group are few .

257
00:32:18,115 --> 00:32:27,327
Cerebral Venous Thrombosis In one study in the United States, 6 percent of cerebral venous thromboses were associated with pregnancy .

258
00:32:27,327 --> 00:32:37,902
However, in more than 8 million deliveries from the Nationwide Inpatient Sample, cerebral venous thrombosis caused only 2 percent of pregnancy-related strokes .

259
00:32:37,902 --> 00:32:45,452
Predisposing causes are numerous, and for gravidas, late pregnancy and the puerperium are times of greatest risk .

260
00:32:45,452 --> 00:32:58,327
Thrombosis of the lateral or superior sagittal venous sinus usually occurs in the puerperium and often in association with preeclampsia, sepsis, or thrombophilia (see Fig. 63-2).

261
00:32:58,327 --> 00:33:04,515
It is more common in patients with inherited thrombophilias or antiphospholipid antibodies.

262
00:33:04,515 --> 00:33:12,177
Headache is the most frequent presenting symptom, neurological deficits are common, and up to a third of women have seizures .

263
00:33:12,177 --> 00:33:21,602
Oh and colleagues (2020) described a postpartum case in which cerebral vein thrombosis was misdiagnosed as postdural puncture headache.

264
00:33:21,602 --> 00:33:25,127
The diagnosis is made using MR venography.

265
00:33:25,127 --> 00:33:35,102
Management includes anticonvulsants for seizures, and anticoagulation with heparin (Pacheco, 2019; Smith, 2018).

266
00:33:35,102 --> 00:33:44,527
Antimicrobials are given for septic thrombophlebitis, and fibrinolytic therapy is reserved for those women failing systemic anticoagulation.

267
00:33:44,527 --> 00:33:50,227
The acute prognosis for venous thrombosis in gravidas is better than in nonpregnant

268
00:33:50,380 --> 00:33:57,267
subjects, and mortality rates are <10 percent (McCau11ey, 2011).

269
00:33:57,217 --> 00:34:10,992
In women with a prior cerebral venous thrombosis, one systematic review found only one recurrence in 217 pregnancies and five noncerebral venous thrombotic events in 186 pregnancies .

270
00:34:10,992 --> 00:34:21,067
In a study of 52 women on prophylactic anticoagulation with prior cerebral venous thrombosis, recurrent thrombosis or bleeding did not develop.

271
00:34:21,067 --> 00:34:25,842
However, 24 percent had late obstetrical complications .

272
00:34:25,842 --> 00:34:36,430
Ischemic Stroke Recurrence Women with prior ischemic stroke have a low risk for recurrence during a subsequent pregnancy unless a specific, persistent cause is identified.

273
00:34:36,430 --> 00:34:50,342
During a 5-year follow-up of 441 reproductive-aged women with prior ischemic stroke or cerebral venous thrombosis, 13 had a recurrent ischemic stroke, and only two were associated with pregnancy.

274
00:34:50,342 --> 00:34:58,380
The authors concluded that the risk of stroke recurrence is low and a previous ischemic stroke is not a contraindication to pregnancy .

275
00:34:58,380 --> 00:35:09,942
In one study of nonpregnant women, the ischemic stroke recurrence rate was similar between those with and without antiphospholipid antibodies as long as the former received warfarin or aspirin .

276
00:35:09,942 --> 00:35:17,492
The recurrence rate of reversible cerebral vasoconstriction syndrome in a subsequent pregnancy was noted to be 9 percent .

277
00:35:17,492 --> 00:35:23,455
Currently, no firm guidelines define prophylaxis in pregnant women with a stroke history .

278
00:35:23,455 --> 00:35:30,830
The American Heart Association stresses the importance of controlling risk factors such as hypertension and diabetes .

279
00:35:30,830 --> 00:35:41,617
As discussed in Chapters 52 and 55, those with antiphospholipid syndrome or certain cardiac conditions should be considered for prophylactic anticoagulation.

280
00:35:41,617 --> 00:35:47,167
For most of these women, lowdose aspirin given throughout pregnancy seems reasonable.

281
00:35:47,167 --> 00:35:49,755
# Hemorrhagic Stroke.

282
00:35:49,755 --> 00:36:04,680
Intracerebral Hemorrhage The symptoms of a hemorrhagic stroke are similar to those of an ischemic stroke, and their differentiation is possible only with CT or MR imaging (Hemphill, 2015; Smith, 2018).

283
00:36:04,680 --> 00:36:12,292
The two distinct categories of spontaneous intracranial bleeding are subarachnoid hemorrhage and intracerebral hemorrhage.

284
00:36:12,292 --> 00:36:16,380
Intracerebral hemorrhage is bleeding into the brain parenchyma.

285
00:36:16,380 --> 00:36:26,567
It most often stems from the spontaneous rupture of Charcot-Bouchard aneurysms of small vessels damaged by chronic hypertension (see Fig. 63-2).

286
00:36:26,567 --> 00:36:42,017
Thus, pregnancy-associated hemorrhagic strokes such as the one shown in Figure 63-3 are often associated with chronic hypertension and superimposed preeclampsia (Cunningham, 2005; Martin, 2005).

287
00:36:42,017 --> 00:36:49,805
Because of its location, this type of hemorrhage has much higher morbidity and mortality rates than subarachnoid hemorrhage .

288
00:36:49,805 --> 00:36:57,455
Pressure-induced rupture causes bleeding into the putamen, thalamus, adjacent white matter, pons, and cerebellum.

289
00:36:57,455 --> 00:37:06,180
In the 28 women described by Martin and associates (2005), half died and most survivors had permanent disabilities.

290
00:37:06,180 --> 00:37:12,792
This underscores proper management of gestational hypertension, especially systolic hypertension.

291
00:37:12,792 --> 00:37:15,392
Figure 63-3.

292
00:37:15,392 --> 00:37:23,967
A noncontrast computed tomography axial head image demonstrates a large intraparenchymal hemorrhage in this woman with preeclampsia.

293
00:37:23,967 --> 00:37:37,030
Subarachnoid Hemorrhage In a study of 639 cases of pregnancy-related subarachnoid hemorrhage from the Nationwide Inpatient Sample, the incidence was 8.5 per 100,000 pregnancies .

294
00:37:37,030 --> 00:37:41,442
A remarkably similar incidence was reported in Japanese women .

295
00:37:41,442 --> 00:37:51,280
These bleeding events are more likely caused by an underlying cerebrovascular malformation in an otherwise normal patient (see Fig. 63-2).

296
00:37:51,280 --> 00:37:57,467
Ruptured saccular or “berry” aneurysms cause 80 percent of all subarachnoid hemorrhages .

297
00:37:57,467 --> 00:38:07,930
The remaining cases stem from a ruptured AVM, coagulopathy, angiopathy, venous thrombosis, infection, drug abuse, tumors, or trauma.

298
00:38:07,930 --> 00:38:16,592
The mortality rate of subarachnoid hemorrhage during pregnancy is lower than that reported for nonpregnant women—8 versus 17 percent .

299
00:38:16,592 --> 00:38:19,017
Intracranial Aneurysm.

300
00:38:19,017 --> 00:38:23,092
Approximately 1 to 2 percent of adults have this lesion .

301
00:38:23,092 --> 00:38:25,855
Only a small percentage rupture.

302
00:38:25,855 --> 00:38:34,517
The rate approximates 0.1 percent for aneurysms <10 mm and 1 percent for those >10 mm .

303
00:38:34,517 --> 00:38:38,817
Most aneurysms identified during pregnancy arise from the

304
00:38:38,980 --> 00:38:43,967
circle of Willis, and in 20 percent of cases, lesions are multiple.

305
00:38:43,917 --> 00:38:47,917
Pregnancy does not raise the risk for aneurysmal rupture.

306
00:38:47,917 --> 00:38:59,842
However, because of their high population prevalence, they are more likely to cause subarachnoid bleeding than other etiologies (Hirsch, 2009; Tiel Groenestege, 2009).

307
00:38:59,842 --> 00:39:11,767
A systematic review of 44 women with 50 aneurysms in pregnancy reported that 72 percent ruptured during pregnancy, and 78 percent of these did so during the third trimester .

308
00:39:11,767 --> 00:39:16,492
This proclivity for rupture late in pregnancy is reported by others .

309
00:39:16,492 --> 00:39:30,242
The cardinal symptom of aneurysmal rupture leading to subarachnoid hemorrhage is sudden severe headache that is accompanied by visual changes, cranial nerve abnormalities, focal neurological deficits, and altered consciousness.

310
00:39:30,242 --> 00:39:40,980
Signs of meningeal irritation, nausea and vomiting, tachycardia, transient hypertension, low-grade fever, leukocytosis, and proteinuria are typical.

311
00:39:40,980 --> 00:39:46,205
Prompt diagnosis and treatment may prevent potentially lethal complications.

312
00:39:46,205 --> 00:39:56,042
The American Heart Association recommends noncontrast cranial CT imaging as the first diagnostic test, although MR imaging may be superior .

313
00:39:56,042 --> 00:40:04,442
Treatment of subarachnoid hemorrhage includes bed rest, analgesia, neurological monitoring, and strict blood pressure control.

314
00:40:04,442 --> 00:40:11,892
Repair of a potentially accessible aneurysm during pregnancy weighs surgical risks against the risk of recurrent hemorrhage.

315
00:40:11,892 --> 00:40:21,530
At least in nonpregnant patients, the risk of subsequent bleeding with conservative treatment is 20 to 30 percent for the first month and then 3 percent per year.

316
00:40:21,530 --> 00:40:29,280
The risk of rebleeding is highest within the first 24 hours, and recurrent hemorrhage leads to death in 70 percent .

317
00:40:29,280 --> 00:40:36,305
Early repair after the sentinel hemorrhage is accomplished by open craniotomy or endovascular coil placement.

318
00:40:36,305 --> 00:40:44,817
In one systematic review of management in pregnancy, coil embolization yielded lower complication rates than aneurysmal clipping .

319
00:40:44,817 --> 00:40:51,805
For unruptured aneurysms, surgical management resulted in a third fewer complications than no treatment.

320
00:40:51,805 --> 00:40:57,742
For gravidas remote from term, repair without hypotensive anesthesia seems optimal.

321
00:40:57,742 --> 00:41:03,980
For women near term, cesarean delivery followed by aneurysm repair is a consideration.

322
00:41:03,980 --> 00:41:07,505
We have successfully done this in several cases.

323
00:41:07,505 --> 00:41:15,917
For aneurysms repaired before or during pregnancy, most allow vaginal delivery if labor ensues remote from aneurysmal repair.

324
00:41:15,917 --> 00:41:23,305
Problems arise in defining “remote,” and although some recommend 2 months, the time for complete healing is unknown.

325
00:41:23,305 --> 00:41:35,142
For women who survive subarachnoid hemorrhage, but in whom surgical repair is not done, we agree with Cartlidge (2000) and recommend against bearing down and favor cesarean delivery.

326
00:41:35,142 --> 00:41:38,017
Arteriovenous Malformations.

327
00:41:38,017 --> 00:41:47,867
These are congenital focal abnormal conglomerations of dilated arteries and veins with subarteriolar disorganization (see Fig. 63-2).

328
00:41:47,867 --> 00:41:52,367
They lack capillaries and have resultant arteriovenous shunting.

329
00:41:52,367 --> 00:41:58,142
Although unclear, the risk of bleeding during pregnancy may rise with gestational age.

330
00:41:58,142 --> 00:42:06,317
When AVMs bleed, half do so into the subarachnoid space, whereas half are intraparenchymal with subarachnoid extension .

331
00:42:06,317 --> 00:42:12,630
Uncommon, they affect an estimated 0.01 percent of the general population.

332
00:42:12,630 --> 00:42:23,780
Of identified AVM cases in pregnancy, 83 percent ruptured during pregnancy or postpartum, and more than 80 percent of these ruptured in the second or third trimester.

333
00:42:23,780 --> 00:42:30,067
Hemorrhage is associated with poor maternal outcome but does not appear to be more likely during pregnancy.

334
00:42:30,067 --> 00:42:38,255
Although correspondingly rare during pregnancy, AVM bleeding accounted for 17 percent of hemorrhagic strokes in one study .

335
00:42:38,255 --> 00:42:52,342
At Parkland Hospital in a 35-year period and nearly 500,000 births, 62 women had a stroke, and five of these were due to a bleeding AVM (Simolke, 1991; Zofkie, 2018).

336
00:42:52,342 --> 00:42:56,867
Treatment of AVMs in nonpregnant patients is individualized.

337
00:42:56,867 --> 00:43:01,667
No consensus guides whether all accessible lesions should be resected.

338
00:43:01,667 --> 00:43:12,930
Factors include AVM symptoms; its anatomy and size; an associated aneurysm, which is found in up to 60 percent of cases; and especially, prior AVM bleeding.

339
00:43:12,930 --> 00:43:17,167
After hemorrhage, the risk of recurrent bleeding in unrepaired

340
00:43:17,332 --> 00:43:27,657
lesions is 6 to 20 percent within the first year, and 2 to 4 percent per year thereafter (Friedlander, 2007; Smith, 2018).

341
00:43:27,607 --> 00:43:32,132
The mortality rate from a bleeding AVM is 10 to 20 percent.

342
00:43:32,132 --> 00:43:38,319
In pregnancy, the decision to operate is usually based on neurosurgical considerations.

343
00:43:38,319 --> 00:43:45,794
Because of the high risk of recurrent hemorrhage from an unresected or inoperable lesion, we favor cesarean delivery.

344
00:43:45,794 --> 00:43:49,082
Demyelinating Or Degenerative Diseases.

345
00:43:49,082 --> 00:43:59,544
The demyelinating diseases are neurological disorders characterized by immune-mediated focal or patchy destruction of myelin sheaths accompanied by an inflammatory response.

346
00:43:59,544 --> 00:44:05,694
The degenerative diseases are multifactorial and characterized by progressive neuronal death.

347
00:44:05,694 --> 00:44:08,419
# Multiple Sclerosis.

348
00:44:08,419 --> 00:44:16,232
This autoimmune demyelinating disease is second only to trauma as a cause of neurological disability in middle adulthood.

349
00:44:16,232 --> 00:44:24,882
The demyelinating effects stem predominantly from T cell–mediated autoimmune destruction of oligodendrocytes that synthesize myelin.

350
00:44:24,882 --> 00:44:37,207
It has a familial occurrence with an environmental trigger, it affects women twice as often as men, and it usually begins in the 20s and 30s (Cree, 2018; Fang, 2020).

351
00:44:37,207 --> 00:44:48,019
In one study, 0.03 percent of California deliveries were complicated by multiple sclerosis (MS) between 2001 and 2009 .

352
00:44:48,019 --> 00:44:58,694
There are three clinical types of multiple sclerosis: 1. Relapsing MS (RMS) is the initial presentation in 90 percent of affected individuals.

353
00:44:58,694 --> 00:45:06,957
With it, unpredictable recurrent episodes of focal or multifocal neurological dysfunction usually are followed by full recovery.

354
00:45:06,957 --> 00:45:11,657
Over time, however, relapses lead to persistent deficits.

355
00:45:11,657 --> 00:45:21,032
2. Secondary progressive MS (SPMS) is relapsing disease that begins to pursue a progressive downhill course after each relapse.

356
00:45:21,032 --> 00:45:24,694
All patients likely develop this type eventually.

357
00:45:24,694 --> 00:45:30,844
3. Primary progressive MS (PPMS) accounts for 10 percent of cases.

358
00:45:30,844 --> 00:45:36,244
With it, disability gradually progresses from the time of initial diagnosis.

359
00:45:36,244 --> 00:45:44,394
Classic findings of MS include sensory loss, visual symptoms from optic neuritis, weakness, and paresthesias.

360
00:45:44,394 --> 00:45:50,332
Clinical diagnosis is aided by MR imaging and cerebrospinal fluid analysis.

361
00:45:50,332 --> 00:46:00,219
In greater than 95 percent of cases, MR imaging shows characteristic multifocal white-matter plaques that represent discrete areas of demyelination .

362
00:46:00,219 --> 00:46:05,044
Their appearance and extent are less helpful for predicting treatment response.

363
00:46:05,044 --> 00:46:07,707
Figure 63-4.

364
00:46:07,707 --> 00:46:12,669
Magnetic resonance cranial images from a woman with multiple sclerosis.

365
00:46:12,669 --> 00:46:21,094
A. T2weighted axial image shows bright signal abnormalities in white matter (arrows), typical for multiple sclerosis.

366
00:46:21,094 --> 00:46:31,944
B. Sagittal T2-FLAIR image shows periventricular hyperintense lesions (Dawson fingers; arrows) representative of demyelination in multiple sclerosis.

367
00:46:31,944 --> 00:46:40,507
Formerly considered an MS variant, neuromyelitis optica spectrum disorder (NMOSD) is viewed as a distinct autoimmune disease.

368
00:46:40,507 --> 00:46:47,257
Antibodies target astrocytes and lead to autoimmune inflammation of the spinal cord and optic nerve.

369
00:46:47,257 --> 00:46:51,544
Some evidence suggests that pregnancy worsens this disorder .

370
00:46:51,544 --> 00:47:01,032
Multiple Sclerosis in Pregnancy Women with MS experience fewer attacks during pregnancy, but with a significantly greater relapse rate postpartum .

371
00:47:01,032 --> 00:47:08,957
This may be related to higher pregnancy-induced numbers of T-helper lymphocytes and an increased T2/Tl ratio.

372
00:47:08,957 --> 00:47:24,832
In a metaanalysis of women with more than 1200 pregnancies complicated by MS, their relapse rate was 0.4 per year before pregnancy; 0.26 per year during pregnancy; and 0.7 per year after delivery .

373
00:47:24,832 --> 00:47:28,507
A systematic review reported similar findings .

374
00:47:28,507 --> 00:47:38,394
Factors associated with postpartum relapse include a high relapse rate before pregnancy, relapses during pregnancy, and a high MS disability score .

375
00:47:38,394 --> 00:47:46,469
Breastfeeding has no effect on postpartum relapses (Hellwig, 2015; Portaccio, 2011).

376
00:47:46,469 --> 00:47:56,057
With uncomplicated disease, pregnancy outcomes are usually not adversely affected (Bove, 2014; Fong, 2018).

377
00:47:56,057 --> 00:48:08,544
Some women may fatigue more easily, those with bladder dysfunction are predisposed to urinary infection, and women with spinal lesions at or above T6 are at risk for autonomic dysreflexia.

378
00:48:08,544 --> 00:48:15,082
In one study of 449 pregnancies in affected women, the labor induction rate was

379
00:48:15,244 --> 00:48:18,569
higher, and second-stage labor was longer .

380
00:48:18,519 --> 00:48:25,331
The greater induction rate and elective operations contributed to the overall higher cesarean delivery rate.

381
00:48:25,331 --> 00:48:34,356
In an analysis of 649 affected women, the mean birthweight was lower, but the perinatal mortality rate was similar to controls .

382
00:48:34,356 --> 00:48:43,506
In a study of 3875 pregnancies in women with MS, risks for infections and preterm delivery were increased .

383
00:48:43,506 --> 00:48:52,694
Management Goals in pregnancy are to arrest acute or initial attacks, employ diseasemodifying agents, and provide symptomatic relief.

384
00:48:52,694 --> 00:48:56,969
Some treatments may require modification during pregnancy.

385
00:48:56,969 --> 00:49:06,519
Acute or initial attacks are treated with high-dose intravenous methylprednisolone—500 to 1000 mg daily for 3 to 5 days.

386
00:49:06,519 --> 00:49:10,181
This is followed by oral prednisone for 2 weeks.

387
00:49:10,181 --> 00:49:16,206
Plasma exchange may help those with fulminant attacks resistant to corticosteroid therapy.

388
00:49:16,206 --> 00:49:20,156
Symptomatic relief can be provided by analgesics.

389
00:49:20,156 --> 00:49:40,269
Carbamazepine, phenytoin, or amitriptyline are suitable after the first trimester for neurogenic pain; baclofen, for spasticity; α1-adrenergic blocking agents, for bladder neck relaxation; and cholinergic and anticholinergic drugs, for bladder-contraction stimulation or inhibition, respectively.

390
00:49:40,269 --> 00:49:47,181
Several disease-modifying therapies are now available to treat relapsing MS or for exacerbations.

391
00:49:47,181 --> 00:49:59,456
Examples include interferons, glatiramer, fingolimod, dimethyl fumarate, natalizumab, and ocrelizumab (Cree, 2018; Tintore, 2019).

392
00:49:59,456 --> 00:50:03,144
Data regarding their safety in pregnancy are limited .

393
00:50:03,144 --> 00:50:09,556
In 246 women with MS, glatiramer was not teratogenic to their offspring .

394
00:50:09,556 --> 00:50:17,831
In a review of 92 pregnancies, first-trimester natalizumab exposure was associated with an increased miscarriage risk .

395
00:50:17,831 --> 00:50:26,456
Fetal exposure in 89 pregnancies to fingolimod was associated with six fetal malformations and nine spontaneous losses.

396
00:50:26,456 --> 00:50:33,019
Because of this and associated animal teratogenicity, its use in pregnancy is not recommended.

397
00:50:33,019 --> 00:50:39,906
Due to its prolonged persistence, contraception is recommended for 2 months after fingolimod cessation .

398
00:50:39,906 --> 00:50:47,206
Last, prevention of relapses postpartum has been reported with intravenous immunoglobulin (IVIG).

399
00:50:47,206 --> 00:50:54,819
A dose of 0.4 g/kg is given daily for 5 days during weeks 1, 6, and 12 .

400
00:50:54,819 --> 00:50:57,631
# Myasthenia Gravis.

401
00:50:57,631 --> 00:51:05,481
This autoimmune-mediated neuromuscular disorder is more common in women, and its incidence peaks in their 20s and 30s .

402
00:51:05,481 --> 00:51:10,431
The etiology is unknown, but genetic factors likely play a role.

403
00:51:10,431 --> 00:51:16,931
Of affected patients, 80 to 90 percent demonstrate antibodies to the acetylcholine receptor .

404
00:51:16,931 --> 00:51:28,619
Those that are seronegative often have antibodies to muscle-specific tyrosine kinase (MuSK), which regulates assembly of the acetylcholine receptor subunits at the neuromuscular junction.

405
00:51:28,619 --> 00:51:37,119
Cardinal features of myasthenia are weakness and easy fatigability of facial, oropharyngeal, extraocular, and limb muscles.

406
00:51:37,119 --> 00:51:40,081
Deep-tendon reflexes are preserved.

407
00:51:40,081 --> 00:51:45,769
Cranial muscles are involved early and disparately, and diplopia and ptosis are common.

408
00:51:45,769 --> 00:51:50,981
Facial muscle weakness causes difficulty in smiling, chewing, and speaking.

409
00:51:50,981 --> 00:51:55,744
In 85 percent of patients, the weakness becomes generalized.

410
00:51:55,744 --> 00:52:01,394
Other autoimmune diseases may coexist, and hypothyroidism should be excluded.

411
00:52:01,394 --> 00:52:04,969
The clinical course is marked by exacerbations.

412
00:52:04,969 --> 00:52:15,644
These can be life threatening and include myasthenic crises, which are characterized by severe muscle weakness, inability to swallow, and respiratory muscle paralysis .

413
00:52:15,644 --> 00:52:26,356
Another is a cholinergic crisis, which is caused by excessive cholinergic medication and leads to nausea, vomiting, muscle weakness, abdominal pain, and diarrhea.

414
00:52:26,356 --> 00:52:30,806
Management Myasthenia gravis is manageable but not curable.

415
00:52:30,806 --> 00:52:36,531
The oral anticholinesterase pyridostigmine (Mestinon) is first-line treatment.

416
00:52:36,531 --> 00:52:44,669
Thymectomy is recommended but postponed until after pregnancy (Amato, 2018a; Wolfe, 2019).

417
00:52:44,669 --> 00:52:52,994
Anticholinesterase medications improve symptoms by impeding acetylcholine degradation but seldom produce normal muscle function.

418
00:52:52,994 --> 00:53:01,981
Ironically, overdose is manifest by the cholinergic crisis mentioned earlier and may be difficult to differentiate from myasthenic symptoms.

419
00:53:01,981 --> 00:53:08,619
Most gravidas refractory to anticholinesterase therapy respond to immunosuppressive therapy with

420
00:53:08,788 --> 00:53:13,050
glucocorticoids, azathioprine, or cyclosporine.

421
00:53:13,000 --> 00:53:27,763
When short-term, rapid improvement is needed—such as for a surgical procedure or a myasthenic crisis—high-dose IVIG or plasma exchange is usually effective (Amato, 2018a; Sanders, 2016).

422
00:53:27,763 --> 00:53:34,525
Myasthenia Gravis and Pregnancy The greatest period of risk is within the first year following diagnosis.

423
00:53:34,525 --> 00:53:39,538
Thus, postponing pregnancy until improvement is sustained is reasonable.

424
00:53:39,538 --> 00:53:47,850
Antepartum management of myasthenia gravis includes close observation, liberal rest, and prompt treatment of infection .

425
00:53:47,850 --> 00:53:54,488
Women in remission who become pregnant while taking corticosteroids or azathioprine should continue these.

426
00:53:54,488 --> 00:54:01,675
Acute onset or exacerbation of myasthenia gravis demands prompt hospitalization and supportive care.

427
00:54:01,675 --> 00:54:06,275
Plasmapheresis and high-dose IVIG are options for emergencies .

428
00:54:06,275 --> 00:54:12,025
In refractory cases, thymectomy has been successfully performed during pregnancy.

429
00:54:12,025 --> 00:54:24,138
Although pregnancy does not appear to affect the overall course of myasthenia gravis, fatigue common to most pregnancies may be exacerbated, and the expanding uterus may compromise respiration.

430
00:54:24,138 --> 00:54:30,188
Maternal hypotension or hypovolemia are ideally avoided because they can trigger crises.

431
00:54:30,188 --> 00:54:37,863
The clinical course of myasthenia gravis during pregnancy is unpredictable, and frequent hospitalizations are the norm.

432
00:54:37,863 --> 00:54:49,975
Up to a third of women have worsening symptoms during pregnancy, and exacerbations develop equally in all three trimesters (Djelmis, 2002; Podciechowski, 2005).

433
00:54:49,975 --> 00:54:57,813
In women with stable disease, most will remain stable throughout pregnancy but likely worsen in the first few months postpartum .

434
00:54:57,813 --> 00:55:03,300
Myasthenia gravis has no significant adverse effects on pregnancy outcomes .

435
00:55:03,300 --> 00:55:14,013
Preeclampsia is a concern because magnesium sulfate may precipitate a severe myasthenia crisis (Hamaoui, 2009; Heaney, 2010).

436
00:55:14,013 --> 00:55:20,463
Although phenytoin use also is problematic in this regard, its adverse effects are less troublesome.

437
00:55:20,463 --> 00:55:25,275
Because smooth muscle is unaffected, most women have normal labor.

438
00:55:25,275 --> 00:55:32,963
Oxytocin is given for the usual indications, and cesarean delivery is reserved for obstetrical indications.

439
00:55:32,963 --> 00:55:41,338
Narcotics may cause respiratory depression, and close observation and respiratory support are essential during labor and delivery.

440
00:55:41,338 --> 00:55:44,063
Curariform drugs are avoided.

441
00:55:44,063 --> 00:55:51,750
Examples include magnesium sulfate, muscle relaxants used with general anesthesia, and aminoglycosides.

442
00:55:51,750 --> 00:55:56,775
Neuraxial analgesia is accomplished with amide-type local agents.

443
00:55:56,775 --> 00:56:07,788
Regional analgesia is preferred unless bulbar involvement or respiratory compromise is significant (Almeida, 2010; Blichfeldt-Lauridsen, 2012).

444
00:56:07,788 --> 00:56:15,825
During secondstage labor, some women may have impaired voluntary expulsive efforts that may warrant operative vaginal delivery.

445
00:56:15,825 --> 00:56:26,675
Neonatal Effects As discussed, 80 percent of mothers with myasthenia gravis have immunoglobulin G (IgG) antibodies against the acetylcholine receptor.

446
00:56:26,675 --> 00:56:32,850
These and anti-MuSK antibodies cross transplacentally, and the fetus can be affected.

447
00:56:32,850 --> 00:56:36,350
Poor fetal swallowing may yield hydramnios .

448
00:56:36,350 --> 00:56:41,950
Similarly, 10 to 20 percent of neonates manifest myasthenia symptoms .

449
00:56:41,950 --> 00:56:48,263
Transient symptoms usually include a feeble cry, poor suckling, and respiratory distress.

450
00:56:48,263 --> 00:56:56,513
Symptoms usually respond to anticholinesterase agents and resolve within a few weeks as maternal IgG antibodies clear.

451
00:56:56,513 --> 00:56:58,413
Neuropathies.

452
00:56:58,413 --> 00:57:05,513
Peripheral neuropathy is a general term for peripheral nerve(s) disorders, in which one or more nerves are affected.

453
00:57:05,513 --> 00:57:12,563
Mononeuropathies are relatively common in pregnancy and signify focal involvement of a single nerve trunk.

454
00:57:12,563 --> 00:57:17,688
These imply local causation such as trauma, compression, or entrapment.

455
00:57:17,688 --> 00:57:25,788
For example, traumatic pudendal, obturator, femoral, and common fibular mononeuropathies can stem from childbirth .

456
00:57:25,788 --> 00:57:32,238
Polyneuropathies can be axonal or demyelinating and can be acute, subacute, or chronic .

457
00:57:32,238 --> 00:57:41,413
These are often associated with systemic diseases such as diabetes, with drug or environmental toxin exposure, or with genetic disease.

458
00:57:41,413 --> 00:57:44,488
Late-stage syphilis is a rare cause .

459
00:57:44,488 --> 00:57:47,263
# Guillain–Barré Syndrome.

460
00:57:47,263 --> 00:57:56,775
In 75 percent of cases, this acute demyelinating polyradiculoneuropathy has clinical or serological evidence of an acute infection.

461
00:57:56,775 --> 00:57:58,263
Common

462
00:57:58,420 --> 00:58:09,120
associated infections are Campylobacter jejuni, cytomegalovirus, Zika virus, and Epstein-Barr virus; surgical procedures; and immunizations .

463
00:58:09,070 --> 00:58:15,607
Guillain–Barré syndrome is thought to be immune-mediated from antibodies formed against nonself antigens.

464
00:58:15,607 --> 00:58:23,495
Demyelination causes sensory and motor conduction blockade, and remyelination yields recovery in most cases.

465
00:58:23,495 --> 00:58:30,620
Clinical features include an areflexic, ascending paralysis, with or without sensory disturbances.

466
00:58:30,620 --> 00:58:33,357
Autonomic dysfunction is common.

467
00:58:33,357 --> 00:58:36,995
The full syndrome develops over 1 to 3 weeks.

468
00:58:36,995 --> 00:58:46,707
Some manifest as chronic inflammatory demyelinating polyneuropathy, and our experiences indicate that this may be relatively common in these young women.

469
00:58:46,707 --> 00:58:54,245
Guillain-Barré syndrome is not more common in pregnancy, and its clinical course mirrors that for nonpregnant individuals.

470
00:58:54,245 --> 00:59:02,045
After an insidious onset, paresis and paralysis most often continue to ascend to cause ventilatory weakness.

471
00:59:02,045 --> 00:59:10,407
Management is supportive and incorporates venous thromboembolism prophylaxis, pressure ulcer prevention, and enteral nutrition.

472
00:59:10,407 --> 00:59:16,957
In the worsening phase, patients are hospitalized, and a fourth require ventilatory assistance.

473
00:59:16,957 --> 00:59:25,245
IVIG or plasmapheresis are beneficial if begun within 1 to 2 weeks of motor symptoms, but neither decreases mortality rates .

474
00:59:25,245 --> 00:59:35,920
Up to 10 percent of patients deteriorate after initial improvement on therapy, and re-treatment with 2 g/kg IVIG over 5 days is recommended.

475
00:59:35,920 --> 00:59:46,132
Although most patients recover fully within several months to a year, the mortality rate is 5 percent, mainly due to pulmonary complications and arrhythmias .

476
00:59:46,132 --> 00:59:48,395
# Bell Palsy.

477
00:59:48,395 --> 00:59:56,770
This disfiguring palsy is usually a mononeuropathic acute facial paralysis that is relatively common in reproductive-aged women .

478
00:59:56,770 --> 01:00:04,870
It has a female predominance, and rates in pregnancy may be increased (Beal, 2018; Choi, 2020).

479
01:00:04,870 --> 01:00:14,482
The disease is characterized by facial nerve inflammation and often is associated with reactivation of herpes simplex virus or herpes zoster virus.

480
01:00:14,482 --> 01:00:18,157
Other causes are Lyme disease and borreliosis.

481
01:00:18,157 --> 01:00:20,895
Figure 63-5.

482
01:00:20,895 --> 01:00:26,932
Bell facial nerve palsy developing on the day of cesarean delivery for dichorionic twins.

483
01:00:26,932 --> 01:00:34,920
This woman was treated with prednisone and antiviral medication, and 3 weeks postpartum, the palsy had almost resolved.

484
01:00:34,920 --> 01:00:41,270
Bell palsy usually has an abrupt and painful onset with maximum weakness by 48 hours.

485
01:00:41,270 --> 01:00:46,632
In some cases, hyperacusis and loss of taste accompany paralysis .

486
01:00:46,632 --> 01:00:53,745
Management includes supportive care with facial muscle massage and eye protection against corneal injury from drying.

487
01:00:53,745 --> 01:01:07,245
Consensus supports that prednisone, 1 mg/kg given orally daily for 5 days, will improve outcomes and shorten the recovery period (Beal, 2018; Madhok, 2016).

488
01:01:07,245 --> 01:01:16,982
It is controversial whether added valacyclovir or acyclovir will hasten recovery (Beal, 2018; Gagyor, 2019).

489
01:01:16,982 --> 01:01:23,057
It is unclear if pregnancy alters the prognosis for spontaneous facial palsy recovery.

490
01:01:23,057 --> 01:01:32,695
In one study, compared with 90 percent of nonpregnant women and men, only half of pregnant women recovered to a satisfactory level after 1 year .

491
01:01:32,695 --> 01:01:40,820
In another report, pregnancy-associated Bell palsy had worse long-term outcomes compared with those of nonpregnant cohort .

492
01:01:40,820 --> 01:01:55,320
Some prognostic markers for incomplete recovery are bilateral palsy, recurrence in a subsequent pregnancy, greater percentage of nerve function loss, and a faster rate of loss (Cohen, 2000; Gilden, 2004).

493
01:01:55,320 --> 01:02:05,132
Other than a fivefold greater rate for gestational hypertension or preeclampsia, women with Bell palsy do not have increased adverse pregnancy outcomes rates .

494
01:02:05,132 --> 01:02:07,882
# Carpal Tunnel Syndrome.

495
01:02:07,882 --> 01:02:14,470
This syndrome results from compression of the median nerve and is the most frequent mononeuropathy in pregnancy .

496
01:02:14,470 --> 01:02:20,632
Symptoms include burning, numbness, or tingling along the inner half of one or both hands.

497
01:02:20,632 --> 01:02:26,207
Others are wrist pain and numbness extending into the forearm and sometimes into the shoulder.

498
01:02:26,207 --> 01:02:33,207
Symptoms are bilateral in 80 percent of gravidas, and 10 percent have evidence for severe denervation.

499
01:02:33,207 --> 01:02:40,570
Differential diagnosis includes cervical radiculopathy of C6–C7 and de Quervain tendonitis.

500
01:02:40,570 --> 01:02:45,245
The latter is caused by swelling of the conjoined tendons and their sheaths

501
01:02:45,412 --> 01:02:47,749
near the distal radius.

502
01:02:47,699 --> 01:02:51,262
Nerve conduction studies may help to clarify.

503
01:02:51,262 --> 01:03:04,387
In pregnancy, the reported incidence of carpal tunnel syndrome is 7 to 43 percent and varies greatly because the range of symptoms is marked (Meems, 2015; Oliveira, 2019).

504
01:03:04,387 --> 01:03:12,137
Symptomatic treatment with a splint applied to a slightly flexed wrist during sleep lightens pressure and usually provides relief.

505
01:03:12,137 --> 01:03:23,699
Although symptoms typically are self-limited, occasionally surgical decompression and corticosteroid injections are necessary (Keith, 2009; Shi, 2011).

506
01:03:23,699 --> 01:03:29,487
Symptoms may persist in more than half of patients at 1 year and in a third at 3 years .

507
01:03:29,487 --> 01:03:34,962
Meralgia paresthetica is another common pregnancy-induced compression neuropathy .

508
01:03:34,962 --> 01:03:42,124
Compression of the lateral femoral cutaneous nerve creates pain, burning, and numbness on the outside of the thigh.

509
01:03:42,124 --> 01:03:48,899
For treatment, the site at which the nerve crosses the crease in the groin can be injected with corticosteroids.

510
01:03:48,899 --> 01:03:51,924
Oral gabapentin is another treatment.

511
01:03:51,924 --> 01:03:54,174
Spinal Cord Injury.

512
01:03:54,174 --> 01:04:04,612
According to the National Spinal Cord Injury Statistical Center (2021), approximately 18,000 new spinal cord injuries are sustained each year.

513
01:04:04,612 --> 01:04:10,587
The average age is 42 years, and males account for 80 percent of new cases.

514
01:04:10,587 --> 01:04:17,137
Cord injury severity determines the short- and long-term prognosis and pregnancy prognosis.

515
01:04:17,137 --> 01:04:24,099
For women, many have altered sexual function and transient hypothalamic pituitary hypogonadism.

516
01:04:24,099 --> 01:04:33,437
Despite this, pregnancy is not uncommon, and in a review of nearly 2000 women, 2 percent reported pregnancy in the prior 12 months .

517
01:04:33,437 --> 01:04:38,574
Two serious and life-threatening events can complicate spinal cord injuries.

518
01:04:38,574 --> 01:04:49,987
First, if the cord is transected above T10, the cough reflex is impaired, respiratory function may be compromised, and pneumonitis from covert aspiration can be serious.

519
01:04:49,987 --> 01:04:58,562
Pulmonary function tests are considered to assess this risk, and some women may need ventilatory support in late pregnancy or labor.

520
01:04:58,562 --> 01:05:05,249
Second, women with lesions above T5–T6 are at risk for autonomic dysreflexia.

521
01:05:05,249 --> 01:05:13,937
With this, stimuli from structures innervated below the level of the spinal lesion lead to massive, disordered sympathetic stimulation.

522
01:05:13,937 --> 01:05:27,974
Abrupt catecholamine release can prompt vasoconstriction with severe hypertension and symptoms that include throbbing headaches, facial flushing, sweating, bradycardia, tachycardia, arrhythmias, and respiratory distress.

523
01:05:27,974 --> 01:05:32,212
Dysreflexia can be precipitated by various stimuli.

524
01:05:32,212 --> 01:05:45,937
These include distention or manipulation of reproductive tract or lower urinary tract organs or bowel (American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, 2020; Krassioukov, 2009).

525
01:05:45,937 --> 01:05:53,837
In one report, 12 of 15 women at risk for autonomic dysreflexia suffered at least one episode during pregnancy .

526
01:05:53,837 --> 01:05:59,524
Gravidas with spinal cord injury have an increased frequency of other complications.

527
01:05:59,524 --> 01:06:11,024
In a review of 37 affected pregnant women, 30 percent had pyelonephritis, 32 percent had lower urinary tract infections, and 9 percent had pressure sores .

528
01:06:11,024 --> 01:06:16,824
Two women developed autonomic dysreflexia, which caused cerebral hemorrhage in one.

529
01:06:16,824 --> 01:06:21,712
Urinary infections may be related to altered vaginal microbiota .

530
01:06:21,712 --> 01:06:29,224
Bowel dysfunction causes constipation in more than half, and anemia and pressurenecrosis skin lesions are common.

531
01:06:29,224 --> 01:06:32,599
Vaginal delivery is generally successful .

532
01:06:32,599 --> 01:06:36,524
Operative vaginal delivery is frequently necessary.

533
01:06:36,524 --> 01:06:46,124
Because uterine contractions are not affected by spinal cord lesions, labor is usually easy, comparatively painless, and at times precipitous.

534
01:06:46,124 --> 01:06:51,137
If the lesion is below T12, uterine contractions are felt normally.

535
01:06:51,137 --> 01:07:00,837
For lesions above T12, the risk of out-ofhospital delivery is substantial and can be minimized by teaching women to palpate for uterine contractions.

536
01:07:00,837 --> 01:07:06,174
This is especially important because up to 20 percent of women deliver preterm .

537
01:07:06,174 --> 01:07:13,662
Some recommend tocodynamometry and weekly cervical examinations beginning at 28 to 30 weeks’ gestation.

538
01:07:13,662 --> 01:07:22,099
Another reasonable option that we frequently employ at Parkland Hospital is elective hospitalization after 36 to 37 weeks.

539
01:07:22,099 --> 01:07:30,687
Spinal or epidural analgesia extending to T10 prevents autonomic dysreflexia and should be instituted at the start of labor .

540
01:07:30,687 --> 01:07:32,374
If severe

541
01:07:32,524 --> 01:07:39,399
symptoms are identified before epidural placement, steps are taken to abolish the provoking stimulus.

542
01:07:39,349 --> 01:07:44,936
A parenteral antihypertensive agent such as hydralazine or labetalol is given.

543
01:07:44,936 --> 01:07:48,224
Idiopathic Intracranial Hypertension.

544
01:07:48,224 --> 01:07:56,261
Also known as pseudotumor cerebri, this disorder is typified by increased intracranial pressure without hydrocephalus.

545
01:07:56,261 --> 01:08:04,799
The cause is unknown, but it may result from overproduction or underabsorption of cerebrospinal fluid (CSF), or both.

546
01:08:04,799 --> 01:08:22,186
Symptoms include headache in at least 90 percent of cases, visual disturbances such as loss of a visual field or central visual acuity in 70 percent, and commonly occurring papilledema that may be sightthreatening (Heaney, 2010; Kesler, 2013).

547
01:08:22,186 --> 01:08:28,524
Other complaints are stiff neck, back pain, pulsatile tinnitus, and cranial nerve palsies .

548
01:08:28,524 --> 01:08:35,936
The syndrome is often found in young women and is prevalent in those who are obese, who recently gained weight, or both .

549
01:08:35,936 --> 01:08:53,736
Along with symptoms, other criteria for diagnosis include elevated intracranial pressure >25 cm H2O, normal CSF composition, normal cranial CT or MR imaging findings, papilledema, and no evidence for systemic disease.

550
01:08:53,736 --> 01:08:58,349
If papilledema is not present, other criteria are required .

551
01:08:58,349 --> 01:09:05,211
Idiopathic intracranial hypertension is usually self-limited, but long-term vision loss is common .

552
01:09:05,211 --> 01:09:15,761
Visual defects can be prevented by lowering the CSF pressure, and agents include acetazolamide to reduce fluid production, furosemide, or topiramate.

553
01:09:15,761 --> 01:09:18,886
Corticosteroids are now rarely used.

554
01:09:18,886 --> 01:09:28,411
Surgical intervention is occasionally necessary and is accomplished by either lumboperitoneal shunting of spinal fluid or optic nerve sheath fenestration.

555
01:09:28,411 --> 01:09:32,636
Another treatment is stenting of dural venous sinus stenosis .

556
01:09:32,636 --> 01:09:38,736
It is controversial if pregnancy is a risk factor for idiopathic intracranial hypertension.

557
01:09:38,736 --> 01:09:46,049
Certainly, symptoms may first appear in pregnancy, and women previously diagnosed may become symptomatic.

558
01:09:46,049 --> 01:09:52,986
Symptoms usually develop by midpregnancy, tend to be self-limited, and usually resolve postpartum.

559
01:09:52,986 --> 01:09:55,899
Pregnancy does not alter management.

560
01:09:55,899 --> 01:10:01,286
Some recommend serial visualfield testing to forestall permanent vision loss.

561
01:10:01,286 --> 01:10:08,311
In a report of 16 pregnant women, visual-field loss developed in four, and it became permanent in one .

562
01:10:08,311 --> 01:10:15,424
Visual-field loss is often coincident with the development of papilledema, for which acetazolamide is given.

563
01:10:15,424 --> 01:10:21,586
Lee and associates (2005) reported successful treatment of 12 pregnant women.

564
01:10:21,586 --> 01:10:31,286
Although outmoded for treatment of nonpregnant individuals, repeated lumbar punctures are generally successful in providing temporary relief throughout pregnancy.

565
01:10:31,286 --> 01:10:38,536
In some pregnant women, surgical therapy becomes necessary, including optic nerve sheath fenestration .

566
01:10:38,536 --> 01:10:45,249
Pregnancy complications are likely due to associated obesity and not to intracranial hypertension.

567
01:10:45,249 --> 01:10:49,811
Rates of adverse maternal and perinatal outcomes are not increased .

568
01:10:49,811 --> 01:11:00,074
The route of delivery depends on obstetrical indications, and conduction analgesia is safe (Aly, 2007; Karmaniolou, 2011).

569
01:11:00,074 --> 01:11:02,674
Maternal Ventricular Shunts.

570
01:11:02,674 --> 01:11:10,911
Pregnancies in women with previously placed ventricular shunts for obstructive hydrocephalus usually have satisfactory outcomes.

571
01:11:10,911 --> 01:11:16,624
Shunts may be ventriculoperitoneal, ventriculoatrial, or ventriculopleural.

572
01:11:16,624 --> 01:11:21,474
Partial obstruction of a shunt is common, especially late in pregnancy .

573
01:11:21,474 --> 01:11:28,124
In one report of 17 such pregnancies, neurological complications were reported in 13 .

574
01:11:28,124 --> 01:11:39,261
Findings included headaches in 60 percent, nausea and vomiting in 35 percent, lethargy in 30 percent, and ataxia or gaze paresis, each in 20 percent.

575
01:11:39,261 --> 01:11:42,949
Most symptoms respond to conservative management.

576
01:11:42,949 --> 01:11:51,924
However, if CT scanning during symptom evaluation discloses acute hydrocephaly, the shunt is tapped or pumped several times daily.

577
01:11:51,924 --> 01:11:57,786
In some cases, surgical revision is necessary and may be emergently indicated .

578
01:11:57,786 --> 01:12:04,311
Another shunting procedure is placement of an endoscopic third ventriculostomy for hydrocephalus.

579
01:12:04,311 --> 01:12:12,236
With this, a small hole is created in the floor of the third ventricle to allow CSF to flow directly into lower cisterns.

580
01:12:12,236 --> 01:12:19,024
One report described successful results in five gravidas who underwent endoscopic ventriculostomy .

581
01:12:19,024 --> 01:12:20,661
Vaginal

582
01:12:20,812 --> 01:12:28,512
delivery is preferred in women with shunts, and unless there is a meningomyelocele, conduction analgesia is permitted.

583
01:12:28,462 --> 01:12:36,862
Antimicrobial prophylaxis is indicated if the peritoneal cavity is entered for cesarean delivery or puerperal sterilization.

584
01:12:36,862 --> 01:12:39,012
Maternal Brain Death.

585
01:12:39,012 --> 01:12:45,774
Brain death is rare in obstetrics, and only a few institutional brain-death policies address pregnancy .

586
01:12:45,774 --> 01:12:58,887
Life-support systems and parenteral alimentation for up to 20 weeks while awaiting delivery have been described (Hussein, 2006; Powner, 2003; Reinhold, 2019).

587
01:12:58,887 --> 01:13:04,224
Some women were treated with aggressive tocolysis and antimicrobial therapy.

588
01:13:04,224 --> 01:13:15,974
In one review of 17 women with persistent vegetative state who were given various levels of support, five women died after delivery, and most of the others remained in their vegetative state .

589
01:13:15,974 --> 01:13:24,887
The ethical, financial, and legal implications, both civil and criminal, that arise from providing or withdrawing such care are profound .

590
01:13:24,887 --> 01:13:29,462
In some women, perimortem cesarean delivery is performed.

